PTSIP COMMUNITY RECIPROCITY LICENSE
Version 1.0 — 2026-09-14

Copyright (c) 2026 Kinirin

PREAMBLE

PTSIP does not require contribution as a price for access.
It requires reciprocity so that knowledge gained through PTSIP does not become a one-way private benefit.

PTSIP is made publicly available so that individuals, researchers, organizations, and developers using coding agents can inspect it, use it, improve it, and build upon it. The purpose of this License is not primarily to collect license fees. The purpose is to preserve attribution, permit broad practical use, and require reasonable return of PTSIP-related knowledge and improvements when PTSIP is used beyond purely private personal use.

This is a source-available community reciprocity license. It is not intended to represent an OSI-approved Open Source license.

1. DEFINITIONS

1.1 "Licensor" means Kinirin, the original PTSIP author and copyright holder, or a successor copyright holder expressly identified by the PTSIP project.

1.2 "PTSIP" means the Primary Lifecycle Ownership and Responsibility Isolation Policy project and the Work distributed with this License.

1.3 "Work" means the particular PTSIP source distribution, repository revision, package, documentation set, Specification, or other material distributed with this License.

1.4 "Protected PTSIP Material" means copyrightable expression originating in PTSIP, including PTSIP source code, normative Specification text, policy definitions, schemas, registry material, canonical data models, conformance vectors, tests, documentation, and the original selection, coordination, and arrangement of those materials to the extent protected by applicable law.

Protected PTSIP Material does not include abstract ideas, general concepts, procedures, methods of operation, algorithms as such, or independently created material merely because it performs a similar function.

1.5 "PTSIP-Derived Material" means material that copies, translates, ports, transforms, adapts, or substantially incorporates Protected PTSIP Material.

A change of programming language, file layout, names, superficial ordering, formatting, or generation through an AI system does not by itself prevent material from being PTSIP-Derived Material when the protected expression was used as a source.

Functional similarity alone does not automatically make an independent implementation PTSIP-Derived Material.

1.6 "Personal Private Use" means use by a natural person solely for that person's own noncommercial, non-organizational, nonpublic purposes and not on behalf of another individual, business, institution, employer, client, or other legal entity.

1.7 "Institutional Research Use" means use by an organization, research group, educational institution, company research unit, laboratory, or similar body for research, evaluation, education, publication, seminar, or technical study that is not yet being used to develop, operate, maintain, market, sell, support, or materially enable a Commercial Activity.

1.8 "Commercial Purpose" means a purpose intended to create, support, preserve, increase, or obtain direct or indirect economic or business value.

Commercial Purpose includes, without limitation, development or operation of paid products or services, pre-revenue products, free products used for customer acquisition or business growth, advertising-supported services, consulting, investment-funded product development, internal business operations, cost reduction, productivity improvement, development-credit or AI-credit reduction, and work performed for a client or employer.

1.9 "Commercial Use" means intentional use of PTSIP or PTSIP-Derived Material for a Commercial Purpose.

Commercial Use does not depend on revenue, profit, net income, positive cash flow, return on investment, or the ability to measure a financial benefit.

1.10 "AI Use" means intentional use of Protected PTSIP Material for AI training, fine-tuning, retrieval, evaluation, benchmarking, agent development, system prompting, compatibility testing, dataset construction, or other machine-learning or generative-AI activity.

1.11 "Material Finding" means a reasonably confirmed PTSIP-related finding that could materially affect correctness, reliability, policy interpretation, architecture governance, compatibility, conformance, safe use, or meaningful interoperability. Examples include serious false positives or false negatives, policy bypasses, substantial ambiguity, repeated coding-agent misbehavior caused by PTSIP semantics, important compatibility defects, or security-relevant defects in PTSIP itself.

1.12 "Official Cooperation Channel" means a submission channel designated by the PTSIP project for registrations, reports, findings, or contributions. The current channels and submission procedures are listed in COMMERCIAL-USE.md. Changes to submission mechanics do not change the substantive obligations of this License.

2. LICENSE GRANT

Subject to compliance with this License, the Licensor grants You a worldwide, non-exclusive, no-charge copyright license to use, reproduce, inspect, modify, create PTSIP-Derived Material from, publicly display, and distribute the Work and PTSIP-Derived Material.

This grant is conditional on the attribution and reciprocity duties applicable to Your form of use.

Using PTSIP to analyze, govern, test, maintain, or assist another project does not transfer ownership of that other project to PTSIP or the Licensor. PTSIP claims no copyright in independently created user code merely because PTSIP was used during development.

No patent right, trademark ownership, or other intellectual-property right is granted except as expressly stated in this License.

3. ACCEPTANCE

You accept this License when You intentionally exercise permissions granted by it beyond rights You already possess under applicable law.

If You do not accept this License, You may not rely on its permissions.

4. ATTRIBUTION

4.1 Copyright and authorship notices distributed with PTSIP must not be removed, falsified, or replaced in a manner that misrepresents the origin of Protected PTSIP Material.

4.2 Personal Private Use does not require a public attribution display, but notices contained in copies of PTSIP must be retained.

4.3 Public distribution, public presentation, Institutional Research Use, Commercial Use, and public AI-related use must provide reasonably visible and discoverable attribution to PTSIP and its original author.

4.4 Attribution must substantially identify:

PTSIP — Primary Lifecycle Ownership and Responsibility Isolation Policy
Original PTSIP author: Kinirin
Canonical project: https://github.com/Kinirin/PTSIP

Equivalent wording is permitted if it clearly communicates the same origin.

4.5 Use of the PTSIP name for compatibility statements, integrations, research, derived products, or branding is permitted, provided the original PTSIP authorship is acknowledged and the use does not falsely imply official status, certification, sponsorship, or endorsement.

5. PERSONAL PRIVATE USE

Personal Private Use is permitted without registration, periodic reporting, or mandatory publication of private modifications.

A person who later distributes the material publicly, uses it on behalf of an organization, or begins Commercial Use becomes subject to the applicable provisions of this License from that later use.

6. PUBLIC AND COMMUNITY REDISTRIBUTION

6.1 You may publicly distribute unmodified or modified PTSIP and PTSIP-Derived Material if You preserve the required attribution.

6.2 PTSIP-Derived portions distributed to the public must remain available under this License, including their corresponding source form when source form exists.

6.3 A larger work may use different terms for independently created portions, provided those terms do not remove or restrict the rights and obligations that this License applies to the PTSIP-Derived portions.

6.4 You may charge for a larger product, hosting, support, distribution, consulting, or other service that includes PTSIP, but such activity is Commercial Use and must satisfy Section 8.

6.5 Redistribution must not represent a third-party or modified version as an official release of the original PTSIP project unless the Licensor expressly authorizes that representation.

7. RESEARCH, EDUCATION, AND INSTITUTIONAL RECIPROCITY

7.1 Institutional Research Use is permitted without a monetary license fee.

7.2 Institutional Research Use must provide attribution in reasonably discoverable research, technical, educational, seminar, publication, or project documentation.

7.3 If Institutional Research Use continues for more than twelve consecutive months, the user must submit a Research Cooperation Report no later than thirty days after each twelve-month anniversary of that use.

7.4 A Research Cooperation Report must, to the extent reasonably available, identify:
(a) the PTSIP versions or revisions used;
(b) the purpose and approximate duration of the research;
(c) relevant environments, coding agents, tools, or integrations;
(d) compatibility observations;
(e) policy ambiguities, failures, unexpected behavior, or notable successful behavior;
(f) PTSIP-related modifications made by the user; and
(g) concrete improvement suggestions or a truthful statement that no material improvement was identified.

7.5 When material Institutional Research Use concludes, or when a material publication, paper, public presentation, or formal research report based on that use is first released, the user must submit a Research Cooperation Summary within sixty days if no substantially equivalent report has already been submitted for the relevant period.

7.6 A Material Finding discovered through Institutional Research Use must be reported within sixty days after it is reasonably confirmed. Security-sensitive findings may be reported through a designated private mechanism when available.

7.7 A material modification to PTSIP itself that is used in Institutional Research Use and is reasonably capable of improving PTSIP correctness, compatibility, or operation must be offered back to the PTSIP project no later than one hundred twenty days after the modification becomes operational or with the next required Research Cooperation Report, whichever occurs first.

7.8 Institutional Research Use becomes Commercial Use when PTSIP or the research implementation is intentionally used to develop, operate, maintain, support, market, sell, or materially enable a Commercial Activity. Commercial obligations begin on that date. Research obligations already accrued remain due.

8. STANDING COMMERCIAL AUTHORIZATION AND COMMERCIAL COOPERATION DUTY

8.1 Standing Authorization

PTSIP provides Standing Commercial Authorization. Separate case-by-case approval, a signature from the Licensor, and a monetary license fee are not required for Commercial Use that satisfies this Section.

Standing Commercial Authorization applies when the Commercial User:
(a) accepts this License;
(b) completes Commercial Use Registration within the Commercial Adoption Window; and
(c) remains in compliance with the applicable attribution and cooperation duties.

The Licensor's silence or failure to respond to a valid registration does not prevent Standing Commercial Authorization from taking effect.

8.2 Commercial Adoption Window

A Commercial User may begin evaluating, integrating, or adopting PTSIP for a Commercial Purpose before registration.

The Commercial User must submit a valid Commercial Use Registration no later than thirty calendar days after the first intentional Commercial Use.

If registration is completed within that period, Standing Commercial Authorization applies from the first day of that Commercial Use.

The thirty-day period may not be repeatedly restarted, reset, or manipulated to avoid registration or cooperation duties.

If registration is not completed by the deadline, continued Commercial Use is not authorized until valid registration is completed.

8.3 Commercial Use Registration

Registration must identify:
(a) the individual or legal entity using PTSIP;
(b) the product, service, system, business activity, or internal commercial function for which PTSIP is used;
(c) the initial PTSIP version or revision;
(d) the date Commercial Use began;
(e) a responsible cooperation contact or accountable project contact; and
(f) the primary Official Cooperation Channel record for that registration.

Registration does not require disclosure of revenue, profit, customer lists, unrelated proprietary code, or confidential financial information.

8.4 Annual Commercial Cooperation Report

For every continuous twelve-month period of Commercial Use, the Commercial User must submit at least one Commercial Cooperation Report no later than thirty days after the end of that period.

The report must provide substantive, truthful information reasonably obtained through actual use, including:
(a) PTSIP versions or revisions used;
(b) usage scope and approximate duration;
(c) material coding-agent, tool, CI, platform, or integration environments;
(d) compatibility findings and workarounds;
(e) known policy ambiguities or architecture-governance findings;
(f) known false positives, false negatives, incorrect classifications, failures, or policy bypasses;
(g) meaningful improvement suggestions, missing capabilities, or usability observations;
(h) a description of modifications made directly to PTSIP and the status of their upstream submission; and
(i) reasonably shareable aggregated operational observations useful to PTSIP improvement.

A Commercial User may truthfully report that no known defect or improvement was found. The user is not required to invent defects or contributions. However, a statement such as "no feedback" does not by itself satisfy the report when usage, version, environment, integration, and reporting-period information can reasonably be provided.

8.5 Material Findings

A Material Finding confirmed during Commercial Use must be reported within thirty days after reasonable confirmation.

8.6 Commercial PTSIP Modifications

If a Commercial User modifies PTSIP itself and uses that modification in Commercial Use, the PTSIP-specific modification must be offered back to the PTSIP project in source form, as a patch, pull request, equivalent implementation, or sufficiently complete technical contribution.

The submission is due no later than the earlier of:
(a) ninety days after the modified PTSIP implementation enters Commercial Use; or
(b) the due date of the next Commercial Cooperation Report.

This obligation applies to the PTSIP modification. It does not require disclosure of unrelated proprietary product source code.

8.7 Ending Commercial Use

Commercial Use does not end merely because:
(a) revenue is zero;
(b) the activity or company is unprofitable;
(c) expenses exceed revenue;
(d) the PTSIP-attributable financial benefit cannot be measured;
(e) a product is pre-revenue, beta, subsidized, investment-funded, or temporarily free; or
(f) PTSIP is one of many technologies used.

Commercial Use ends only when PTSIP and applicable PTSIP-Derived Material are no longer materially used to develop, operate, maintain, support, improve, distribute, or otherwise enable the relevant Commercial Activity.

8.8 Final Commercial Cooperation Report

When Commercial Use ends, the Commercial User must submit a Final Commercial Cooperation Report within thirty days if material Commercial Use occurred after the period covered by the most recent Commercial Cooperation Report.

Ending Commercial Use does not cancel duties that accrued before Commercial Use ended.

8.9 Resumption

If Commercial Use resumes, the Commercial Cooperation Duty resumes automatically.

The Commercial User must update or renew the applicable registration within thirty days after resumed Commercial Use.

A materially different category of Commercial Use requires an updated or additional registration.

8.10 No Profit-Based Avoidance

For avoidance of doubt, no Commercial User may avoid this Section merely by asserting that the user earned no revenue, operated at a loss, could not isolate PTSIP's financial contribution, had not yet reached profitability, received only indirect economic value, or temporarily provided the relevant activity without charge.

The duty follows Commercial Use, not profitability.

9. AI USE

9.1 AI Use of PTSIP is permitted subject to this License.

9.2 When PTSIP is intentionally used in AI training, fine-tuning, evaluation, dataset construction, agent development, or compatibility work, attribution must be provided in a reasonably discoverable model card, training record, dataset record, system documentation, technical documentation, compliance record, research documentation, or equivalent location.

This License does not require every individual AI output to contain a PTSIP notice.

9.3 Noncommercial Institutional AI Use is subject to Section 7.

9.4 Commercial AI Use is subject to Section 8.

9.5 PTSIP-related findings gained through AI Use, including policy failures, compatibility results, agent-behavior findings, evaluation results, and meaningful improvement observations, must be shared according to the applicable research or commercial reporting duties.

10. INFORMATION PROTECTION AND REASONABLE DISCLOSURE

10.1 This License does not require disclosure of:
(a) personal information that may not lawfully be disclosed;
(b) passwords, credentials, keys, or authentication secrets;
(c) customer-confidential information;
(d) legally privileged or otherwise legally protected information;
(e) information whose disclosure is prohibited by a binding third-party obligation;
(f) trade secrets unrelated to PTSIP itself; or
(g) unrelated proprietary product source code.

10.2 Where raw information cannot reasonably or lawfully be disclosed, the user should provide an anonymized result, aggregated information, reduced reproduction, compatibility matrix, technical summary, or other meaningful substitute when reasonably possible.

10.3 A confidentiality assertion must not be used as a pretext to withhold information that can reasonably be provided without exposing protected information.

11. COMPLIANCE, CURE, SUSPENSION, AND REINSTATEMENT

11.1 Ordinary Noncompliance

Failure to submit a required registration, report, finding, attribution correction, or PTSIP modification by its deadline is Ordinary Noncompliance.

A sixty-day cure period begins on the missed deadline.

Standing Commercial Authorization remains conditionally available during that cure period solely for a user acting in good faith to cure the omission.

If the violation remains uncured when the cure period expires, the affected Commercial Authorization is automatically suspended.

11.2 Effect of Suspension

During suspension, the user may perform only activities reasonably necessary to:
(a) cure the violation;
(b) cease unauthorized PTSIP use;
(c) protect security or data integrity; or
(d) comply with applicable law.

Accrued obligations remain due.

11.3 Automatic Reinstatement

Authorization suspended solely for Ordinary Noncompliance is automatically reinstated when all overdue obligations causing the suspension are fully cured.

Reinstatement does not erase the historical violation and does not reset reporting anniversaries.

11.4 Material or Intentional Noncompliance

Intentional removal or falsification of attribution, knowingly false cooperation reports, deliberate concealment of a Material Finding to avoid reporting, deliberate concealment of a required PTSIP modification, or repeated manipulation of adoption, suspension, or resumption periods to evade reciprocity duties is Material Noncompliance.

For Material Noncompliance, the Licensor may provide written notice requiring cure within thirty days. If the violation is not cured within that period, the Licensor may suspend the affected authorization.

Repeated or persistent Material Noncompliance may require renewed written authorization after cure.

11.5 Persistent Noncompliance

If a required obligation remains uncured for one hundred eighty days after its original due date, the affected Standing Commercial Authorization may be treated as terminated.

A user that fully cures the outstanding obligations may request or obtain a new Standing Commercial Authorization by completing the then-applicable registration requirements.

11.6 No Monetary Penalty Created by This License

This License does not create a fixed license fee, royalty, fine, or liquidated-damages schedule.

Nothing in this Section limits remedies that applicable law may otherwise provide.

12. RECIPROCITY, NOT ACCESS FEES

PTSIP does not require contribution as a price for access.

The reciprocity duties exist so that PTSIP-related knowledge, compatibility findings, corrections, and improvements obtained through organized or commercial use do not remain solely a one-way private benefit.

Commercial Authorization does not inherently require payment of a monetary license fee.

The Licensor and a user may voluntarily enter a separate written agreement for support, consulting, confidentiality, certification, or other services, but such an agreement does not silently replace this License unless it expressly says so.

